  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 PM EDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\n- Becoming Hot and Humid\n\nAn upper-level high will develop over the southern CONUS and \nmigrate toward the southeast CONUS by midweek. Southwesterly low- \nlevel flow from the southern plains will transport 850 mb \ntemperatures around 20 C over Lower Michigan from late Sunday \nthrough Tuesday or Wednesday. At the surface, a warm front in the \narea on Sunday should progress north on Monday, with high \ntemperatures inland favored to reach the upper 80s to lower 90s. \nEnsembles point to Wednesday potentially being the hottest day \nbefore a majority of the solutions shunt the heat south of the \narea on Thursday. With the dew points climbing toward 70 degrees, \nthe heat index could climb to 95-100 degrees Mon-Wed.\n\n- Rain Chances Increase Tonight into Sunday\n\nNorth of a surface warm front tonight into Sunday, a layer of \nmoisture and clouds amid weak instability is streaming in around \n700 mb / 10,000 feet. With relatively drier air beneath, the \ncurrent batch of rain inbound from Wisconsin will weaken to just \nlight rain around Ludington and sprinkles elsewhere this evening. \nAdditional moisture / higher theta-e at that layer advecting in \nlate tonight into Sunday, along with MUCAPE gradually increasing \nabove 500 J/kg, will support the development of numerous showers \nand embedded thunderstorms. DCAPE is relatively weak on Sunday, \nthough with the drier low-level air in place with 35 knot winds a \ncouple thousand feet above the surface, occasional 30-40 mph wind \ngusts could get mixed down on Sunday.\n\n- Additional Chances for Rain and Thunderstorms Next Week\n\nPrecipitation chances are lower on Monday as most of the activity\nshould lift north out of the area with the advancing warm front. \nThe pattern will be rather unsettled through at least Tuesday and \nWednesday as we will be on the northern edge of the heat dome, \nwith quasi-zonal flow that will contain upper-level jet streaks \nand shortwaves. CAPE generally around 1000-2000 J/kg will be \npresent, with the question being when might moisture layers or \nlift mechanisms help break the cap. The timing of such chances for\nstorms is uncertain at this point, though given adequate deep- \nlayer shear also present, a marginal risk of severe weather could \neventually manifest.",
